      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1200,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "NOAA Photo Library",
      "description": "NOAA Administrator Vice Adm. Conrad Lautenbacher (right), NOAA National Marine Sanctuary Program Director Daniel Basta (left), and U.S. Navy Capt. Christopher Murray (center) unveil a NOAA Heritage Marker during the grand opening of the USS Monitor Center at The Mariners' Museum in Newport News, Va. The marker is set beneath a spider-like steel cradle used by NOAA and the Navy in 2002 to recover the USS Monitor's 120-ton gun turret from the sea floor. The recovery operation, which took place in the Monitor National Marine Sanctuary off Cape Hatteras, N.C., involved NOAA marine archaeologists, Mariners' Museum conservators, and more than a hundred Navy divers, some of whom are pictured here.\n\nThe turret and other artifacts recovered from the sunken Civil War ironclad are on public display at the museum.",
